**Tile Grammar**

Six legs and a thorax narrow as a sentence's clause; this is Monomorium pharaonis, or perhaps something smaller, unnamed in my private taxonomy, crossing the grout-line like a comma crosses a sentence—pausing, not pausing, the distinction lost on me at 2 a.m. with the bathroom light too bright and my mind too loose; there is a book on the shelf called *Much Ado About Nothing*, and I think of it now, watching this—this fuss, this parade of a single body multiplied by patience, by pheromone, by the invisible highway laid down in chemicals I cannot see but the ants apparently can, can *read*, the way I read grout-lines as roads, as sentences, as the syntax of something enormous and indifferent;

the exoskeleton catches light like chitin always does, dark and segmented, three parts—head, thorax, abdomen—a trinity as rigid as any doctrine, and I think: *petiole*, I think: *that little waist-node connecting the middle to the end*, a word I learned once and now cannot unlearn, cannot stop seeing in the way this ant's body pinches at the center like punctuation, like a semicolon itself, connecting two clauses that could stand alone but don't, that choose, instead, this fragile joining;

they are always looking for sugar, for the grain of something dropped, for the sweetness that fell from my toothpaste or my late-night cereal bowl, and is this ado about nothing, is this Shakespeare's joke made literal—all this noise, this scurry, this six-legged Beatrice and Benedick bickering across the linoleum for a crumb, for a kingdom the size of a fingernail; I lower my face to the tile, my cheek nearly against the cold ceramic, and I watch the column bend around the drain-cover, the way a river bends around a stone, the way a plot bends around a misunderstanding, and think: *Formicidae*, think: *over twelve thousand species*, think: *how many are in my house right now, in the walls, in the pipes, in the small kingdom I did not know I was hosting*;

is it ado about nothing or is it everything—the whole architecture of a colony, the queen somewhere unseen, laying and laying, her body swollen with a purpose no comedy could satirize; is it nothing, this six-legged sonnet, this tiny iambic walk, da-DUM, da-DUM, antennae testing the air like a blind man's cane, like Gloucester on the heath, like anyone looking for solid ground;

I turn off the light eventually; the ants continue in the dark, I assume, their compound eyes needing no permission from me, no illumination, no audience; and I go to bed thinking of scale, of how much ado can fit inside a body one centimeter long, thinking: this is nothing, this is everything, this is the bathroom floor at 2 a.m., this is Shakespeare and chitin and grout-lines all conspiring toward the same small, persistent noise.
